# BusBunching – Run Commands

Run both commands from the repository root. Arguments in square brackets are optional.

## 1. Run the simulation

python run_simulation.py [traffic|notraffic] [--control] [--holding] [--seed N] [--duration S]

- `traffic` — Includes passenger cars. This is the default.
- `notraffic` — Runs the simulation with buses only.
- `--control` — Enables bus speed control.
- `--holding` — Enables stop holding to enforce a minimum departure headway of 55 seconds.
- `--seed N` — Sets the random seed. Default: `42`.
- `--duration S` — Sets the maximum simulation duration in seconds. Default: `3500`.

example:
python run_simulation.py notraffic --control --holding

## 2. Plot departure headways

python plot_headway.py [traffic|notraffic] [stop] [--control] [--holding]

- `traffic` / `notraffic` — Selects the traffic mode of the simulation results. Default: `traffic`.
- `stop` — Selects a stop from `0` to `7`. Both `3` and `stop_3` select the same stop. Default: `0`.
- `--control` — Selects results from a run with bus speed control.
- `--holding` — Selects results from a run with stop holding.

example:
python plot_headway.py notraffic 0 --control --holding

Use the same traffic mode and control flags for the simulation and the plot. The `--` flags can appear in any order. Keep each value directly after its flag, and put the traffic mode before the stop index.